.TH FUZZYGENE 1 2000-1月 "fuzzygene_relaxation" "fuzzygene_relaxation"
.SH NAME
fuzzygene_relaxation \- Set the damping factor, the iteration budget and the tolerance used by the steady-state solver.
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fIfuzzygene_relaxation(\fBfuzzygene\fR as <generic> FuzzyGeneModel, 
\fBomega\fR as double = 0.5, 
\fBsteps\fR as integer = 50, 
\fBtolerance\fR as double = 1E-06, 
[\fB<Environment>\fR]);\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
Set the damping factor, the iteration budget and the tolerance used by the steady-state solver.
.PP
.SH OPTIONS
.PP
\fBfuzzygene\fB \fR\- a trained (or restored) model. 
.PP
.PP
\fBomega\fB \fR\- damping factor in (0, 1]; 0.5 is the recommended value. 
.PP
.PP
\fBsteps\fB \fR\- maximum iteration steps, must be positive. 
.PP
.PP
\fBtolerance\fB \fR\- convergence threshold on the per step max change, must be positive. 
.PP
.PP
\fBenv\fB \fR\- the R# runtime environment used to report errors. 
.PP
.SH VALUE
.PP
the same model object, so the call can be chained
.PP
.SH DETAILS
.PP
The trajectory produced by \fBfuzzygene_trace_perturbation\fR is the damped fixed-point
 iteration `z <- z + omega * (F(z) - z)`. With \fBomega = 1\fR (no damping) the iteration
 is the plain repeated application of `F`, and on a genome scale model that often keeps
 oscillating instead of converging; \fBomega = 0.5\fR is the usual compromise between stability
 and speed.
 


 


 Always rerun the trajectory after changing these values, and check \fBsummary$converged\fR:
 a non converged run means the flat tail of the curve is a truncated plateau.
 


 
\fB
 model = FuzzyGene::fuzzygene_relaxation(model, omega = 0.5, steps = 80);
 let t = FuzzyGene::fuzzygene_trace_perturbation(model, "TPPP3");
 print(t$summary);
 
\fR`
.PP
